# Implementation Plan: `titleCase`

## Context

Repo is a small ESM fixture (`package.json` has `"type": "module"`, no dependencies, no test runner config). There's a sibling implementation `src/slugify.mjs` (currently a stub throwing "not implemented") that shows the expected file shape: a single named export function per file, no imports. There is no existing `test/` directory. Node version isn't pinned in `package.json`; assume a Node version with built-in `node:test` and `node:assert` (Node 18+).

## Files to create/change

1. **`src/title-case.mjs`** (new file)
   - Export a single named function: `export function titleCase(text) { ... }`.
   - Implementation approach:
     - Split `text` on runs of whitespace using a regex that also trims leading/trailing whitespace, e.g. `text.trim().split(/\s+/)`.
       - Guard the empty-string case: `''.trim().split(/\s+/)` yields `['']`, not `[]`. Handle this explicitly (e.g. return `''` early if `text.trim() === ''`) so titleCase("") and titleCase("   ") return `""` rather than a stray uppercase artifact.
     - For each word, lowercase the whole word first, then uppercase just the first character: `word.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + word.slice(1).toLowerCase()`.
       - Applying `.toLowerCase()` to the remainder (not skipping it) satisfies "the rest of every word is lowercased" even for inputs like `"HELLO"` or `"hELLO"`.
     - Join the transformed words with a single space: `.join(' ')`.
   - No dependencies beyond the standard library (`String.prototype` methods only — no imports needed).
   - No other exports; don't add a default export unless the existing `slugify.mjs` pattern is checked again and shown otherwise (it isn't — it also uses a single named export, so mirror that).

2. **`test/title-case.test.mjs`** (new file, new `test/` directory)
   - Use `node:test` and `node:assert/strict` (or `node:assert`), importing `titleCase` from `../src/title-case.mjs`.
   - Structure: one `test(...)` block per acceptance criterion, plus a couple of combined/edge cases. Suggested cases:
     - `titleCase('hello world')` → `'Hello World'` (basic first-letter-uppercase, multi-word).
     - `titleCase('HELLO WORLD')` → `'Hello World'` (rest of word lowercased even when input is all caps).
     - `titleCase('hELLO wORLD')` → `'Hello World'` (mixed case normalized).
     - `titleCase('hello   world')` (multiple internal spaces) → `'Hello World'` (whitespace run collapses to one space).
     - `titleCase('  hello world  ')` (leading/trailing spaces) → `'Hello World'` (no leading/trailing space in output).
     - `titleCase('hello\tworld\nfoo')` (tab/newline whitespace) → `'Hello World Foo'`, to confirm "runs of whitespace" isn't interpreted as literal-space-only (regex `\s+` covers this; only add this case if the acceptance text is read to include general whitespace — safe either way since `\s` is a superset of space).
     - `titleCase('single')` → `'Single'` (one word, no separators).
     - Optional edge case: `titleCase('')` → `''` (empty input, guards the split-on-empty-string trap noted above).
   - Each `assert.strictEqual`/`assert.equal` compares the function's return value to the expected string exactly (verifies single-space separation and exact casing, not just a loose match).

## Definition of done, mapped to acceptance criteria

- **"titleCase uppercases the first letter of every word"** → done when every test case with multi-word input shows each word's first character capitalized in the asserted expected string, and the assertions pass.
- **"the rest of every word is lowercased"** → done when the `'HELLO WORLD'` / `'hELLO wORLD'` mixed-case test cases pass, proving the implementation lowercases characters after index 0 rather than leaving original casing.
- **"runs of whitespace collapse to one space, with no leading or trailing space"** → done when the multiple-internal-space and leading/trailing-space test cases both pass, and the returned string contains no double spaces and doesn't start/end with a space character (implicit in exact-string equality assertions).
- **"tests pass with `node --test`"** → done when running `node --test` from the repo root (which auto-discovers `test/title-case.test.mjs` by Node's default test file naming convention `*.test.mjs`) exits 0 with all assertions green. Verify by actually running `node --test` after implementation, not just by inspection.

